CORE
main.cpp
--cpp20
^\[main\.assertion\.\d+\] line \d+ fwd-ref pack forwards lvalue value: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.\d+\] line \d+ fwd-ref pack forwards rvalue value: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.\d+\] line \d+ fwd-ref pack preserves lvalue category: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.\d+\] line \d+ fwd-ref pack preserves rvalue category: SUCCESS$
^VERIFICATION SUCCESSFUL$
^EXIT=0$
^SIGNAL=0$
--
^.*dereference failure.*: FAILURE$
^CONVERSION ERROR$
--
C++ [temp.deduct.call]/3 forwarding-reference deduction applied element-wise to
a forwarding-reference parameter pack (Args&&... args).  The deduction code
(cpp_typecheck_resolve.cpp guess_function_template_args) applied the rule only
to a single forwarding reference; for a pack it deduced each element as a plain
rvalue reference, so an lvalue argument bound through a garbage reference and
the wrong value was forwarded (a *a-dereference failure plus a corrupted value).
The fix detects forwarding-reference packs and, per element, deduces "lvalue
reference to A" for lvalue arguments and "A" for rvalue arguments.  This is a
prerequisite for std::__invoke / std::reference_wrapper::operator() (and hence
std::ref'd predicates) to forward correctly.
